---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Term from "../../../components/Term.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology — aiMark"
  description="How aiMark scores work: the 1000-point reference anchor, weighted geometric means, sub-scores, tracks, suite versioning, and honest integrity."
>
  <div class="container container--narrow page">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every aiMark number is designed to be explainable. This page covers how the zero-choice
      benchmark classifies your machine and scores it, why models are test assets rather than
      choices, how cloud APIs fit in, and — in the advanced-mode section — how the underlying
      suite scores are computed and what we do (and honestly cannot do) about gamed results.
    </p>

    <h2 id="zero-choice">The zero-choice benchmark</h2>
    <p>
      Running <code>aimark</code> involves no decisions: the CLI inspects your machine, assigns a
      <Term id="capability-class">capability class</Term>, downloads the pinned
      <Term id="test-asset">test assets</Term> for that class, runs the fixed test program, and
      produces one <Term id="system-score">System Score</Term>. The benchmark measures
      <strong>your system</strong>, the way 3DMark measures a PC — the model is part of the test,
      not the thing being tested.
    </p>

    <h3 id="classes">Capability classes and the classification rule</h3>
    <p>
      The classification input is <strong>usable accelerator memory</strong>: the largest GPU's
      <Term id="vram">VRAM</Term>. Two deliberate wrinkles:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple Silicon <Term id="unified-memory">unified memory</Term> counts at 70%.</strong>
        Unified memory is shared with the OS and everything else running, so a 32 GB M-series Mac
        cannot actually dedicate 32 GB to model weights. Counting 70% puts Macs in the class whose
        test model they can genuinely run well, rather than flattering the spec sheet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CPU-only machines are always Compact, regardless of RAM.</strong> A 64 GB
        CPU-only box can <em>hold</em> a 32B model but cannot meaningfully run one — memory
        bandwidth, not capacity, is the wall. It's the same reason an integrated GPU runs 3DMark
        Night Raid and not Time Spy Extreme.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Class</th>
            <th scope="col">Usable accelerator memory</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Compact</th><td>CPU-only, or under 6 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Mainstream</th><td>6–16 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Performance</th><td>16–24 GB</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Ultra</th><td>24 GB and up</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Each class runs its own pinned test model (one model family across classes, for a clean
      scaling story), and every class <em>also</em> runs the Compact model as a cross-class anchor
      cell — so any two machines share at least one identical workload. System Scores are
      comparable only within a (benchmark version, class) pair; the class leaderboards rank
      <strong>systems</strong>, never models.
    </p>

    <h3 id="test-assets">Models are test assets</h3>
    <p>
      In zero-choice mode, the model is part of the benchmark the way a rendering scene is part of
      3DMark. The program manifest pins exact model files and a specific llama.cpp build by URL
      and sha256; the CLI downloads, verifies, and runs them — your installed runtimes and models
      are not involved. Changing any test asset would silently change what scores mean, so any
      change produces a new program version and a fresh leaderboard, exactly like
      <a href="#versioning">suite versioning</a> below.
    </p>

    <h3 id="validity">Validity checks: graded tasks as tamper detection</h3>
    <p>
      The program includes a graded-quality cell (Gauntlet), but it is a
      <Term id="validity-check">validity check</Term>, not a score input. In a hardware benchmark
      the model's answer quality is a <strong>constant of the class</strong> — every Mainstream
      machine runs the same model on the same tasks, so accuracy should land in the same band
      everywhere. A result far below that band doesn't mean "slow hardware"; it means the run is
      broken or cheated (wrong model, corrupted weights, a doctored client). Such results flag the
      whole benchmark off the default boards instead of moving its score.
    </p>

    <h3 id="cloud-monitoring">Cloud APIs are monitoring, not competition</h3>
    <p>
      Hosted APIs don't race local machines on aiMark. A scheduled monitor probes providers and
      records latency and throughput over time as a <Term id="monitoring-run">reference series</Term>
      — shown on the <a href="/cloud/">cloud monitor</a> page so you can put a local score in
      context. Monitoring probes typically run on CI infrastructure and are
      <em>included</em> there by design (the one place CI-sourced data is the point: the probe
      measures the provider, not the machine sending the request). They are excluded from every
      leaderboard.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2 id="advanced">Advanced mode: pick-your-own suites</h2>
    <p>
      Everything below describes the underlying suite machinery — which both powers the
      zero-choice program's cells <em>and</em> remains directly usable as advanced mode
      (<code>aimark run &lt;suite&gt; --target …</code>) for benchmarking your own model/runtime
      combinations. Advanced-mode runs appear on the <a href="/leaderboard/sprint/1/">per-suite
      boards</a> and feed the <a href="/model-fit/">model-fit matrix</a>, but never the class
      system leaderboards.
    </p>

    <h2 id="score-model">The score model</h2>
    <p>
      aiMark borrows the trick that made 3DMark scores meaningful: every
      <Term id="suite">suite</Term> version ships a frozen
      <Term id="reference-baseline">reference baseline</Term> for each
      <Term id="metric">metric</Term> it measures. Match the reference and you score
      <strong>~1000 points</strong>. Twice as good ≈ 2000. Half as good ≈ 500. The anchor never
      moves within a suite version, so a score means the same thing today and a year from now.
    </p>
    <p>Mechanically, each sub-score is:</p>
    <pre><code>sub_score = 1000 × weighted geomean( metric / reference_metric )</code></pre>
    <p>
      For metrics where lower is better (like <Term id="ttft">TTFT</Term>), the ratio is inverted
      before averaging. The weights and reference values are <strong>data, not code</strong> —
      frozen in the suite manifest, published openly, and identical for every submission.
    </p>

    <h3 id="sub-scores">Four sub-scores</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Sub-score</th>
            <th scope="col">Measures</th>
            <th scope="col">Built from</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Performance</th>
            <td>How fast it feels and finishes</td>
            <td>
              <Term id="decode">decode</Term> tok/s, <Term id="ttft">TTFT</Term>,
              <Term id="latency">latency</Term>
              <Term id="p50">p50</Term>/<Term id="p95">p95</Term>/<Term id="p99">p99</Term>,
              <Term id="throughput">throughput</Term> under <Term id="concurrency"
                >concurrency</Term
              >
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Quality</th>
            <td>Whether answers are right</td>
            <td>Accuracy on objectively-graded tasks — no LLM-as-judge in v1</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Cost-Efficiency</th>
            <td>Quality per dollar (hosted)</td>
            <td>
              <Term id="cost-per-token">Pricing snapshot</Term> captured at run time; local energy
              metering may come later
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consistency</th>
            <td>How steady results are run-to-run</td>
            <td>
              Inverse <Term id="coefficient-of-variation">coefficient of variation</Term> across
              <Term id="repetition">repetitions</Term>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The composite <strong>aiMark Score</strong> is the weighted
      <Term id="geometric-mean">geometric mean</Term> of whichever sub-scores the suite produces.
      Geometric, not arithmetic, on purpose: with a geometric mean you cannot buy a high composite
      by maxing one dimension while tanking another — doubling one sub-score and halving another
      cancels out. Not every suite produces every sub-score; Sprint 1 produces Performance and
      Consistency.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tracks">Tracks: local vs hosted</h2>
    <p>
      Scores are comparable only within a <strong>(suite, version, <Term id="track">track</Term
      >)</strong> triple. The <code>local</code> track benchmarks models on your own machine — the
      <Term id="hardware-profile">hardware profile</Term> is captured, and there is no cost
      dimension. The <code>hosted</code> track benchmarks <Term id="provider">provider</Term> APIs
      over the internet — <Term id="region">region</Term> and pricing are captured, and timing
      includes real network overhead.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two tracks measure genuinely different things, so they are
      <strong>never implicitly compared</strong>. The only place a local run can appear next to a
      hosted run is the <a href="/compare/">compare view</a>, where you asked for it explicitly and
      the caveats are shown.
    </p>

    <h2 id="versioning">Suite versioning — like 3DMark generations</h2>
    <p>
      A <Term id="suite-version">suite version</Term> freezes everything that could affect a score:
      tasks, prompts, grading, reference baselines, weights, and protocol (warmups, repetitions,
      timeouts, decoding settings). <strong>Any</strong> change — even a better prompt — produces a
      new version with a brand-new leaderboard. Old boards stay up, frozen; old scores are never
      silently invalidated or mixed with new ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is exactly how 3DMark generations work, and it is why a "Sprint 1" score will still mean
      something years from now. Sprint 1's reference anchors are synthetic round numbers (100 tok/s
      decode, 50ms TTFT, 1500ms p95 latency, CV 0.10); a future version will re-anchor to a
      measured reference rig — and per the rules above, that resets the board.
    </p>

    <h2 id="captured">What gets captured on every run</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Model:</strong> name, <Term id="parameter-count">parameter count</Term>,
        <Term id="quantization">quantization</Term>, and a <Term id="model-digest"
          >model digest</Term
        > where the runtime provides one
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Execution:</strong> <Term id="runtime">runtime</Term> or provider with version,
        <Term id="region">region</Term> (hosted), <Term id="context-window">context length</Term>,
        <Term id="concurrency">concurrency</Term>, <Term id="prompt-caching">prompt caching</Term> and
        <Term id="streaming">streaming</Term> flags
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hardware (local):</strong> CPU and cores, RAM, GPU and <Term id="vram">VRAM</Term>,
        <Term id="unified-memory">unified memory</Term>, OS — hashed into a
        <Term id="hardware-profile">hardware profile id</Term> so identical rigs cluster
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Provenance:</strong> CLI version, timestamp, pricing snapshot (hosted), and the
        integrity signature
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Because the full settings vector rides along with every score, leaderboards are sliceable by
      any of these dimensions, and corpus-wide
      <Term id="parameter-impact">parameter-impact</Term> analysis can answer questions like
      "how much does quantization actually move Sprint scores?"
    </p>

    <h2 id="integrity">Integrity — the honest version</h2>
    <p>
      Let's be straight about this: <strong
        >a client-side benchmark can never be cryptographically trustworthy.</strong
      > The benchmark runs on hardware you control, with a binary you could modify. Anyone who promises
      otherwise is selling something. Like 3DMark, aiMark's strategy is to raise the bar for cheating
      and rely on statistics to catch what slips through.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong><Term id="hmac-signing">HMAC signing</Term>.</strong> Official release binaries sign
        each result payload, and the API verifies signatures on submission. The key ships inside the
        binary, so a determined person can extract it — we document that openly. Signing stops casual
        tampering, not determined attackers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Environment capture.</strong> Runtime versions, <Term id="model-digest"
          >model digests</Term
        >, and OS fingerprints make fabricated setups harder to describe consistently.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Statistical detection — the real defense.</strong> Every submission is checked
        against per-metric <Term id="plausibility-check">plausibility bounds</Term> and against the
        distribution of comparable runs. Physically impossible values and extreme
        <Term id="outlier">outliers</Term> (beyond ~4σ for their cohort) are automatically flagged —
        kept publicly visible for transparency, but excluded from default boards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><Term id="integrity-tier">Tiers</Term>.</strong> Runs are labelled
        <code>unverified</code> (anonymous), <code>claimed</code> (GitHub-linked), or
        <code>verified</code> (signature + plausibility passed). Default board views favor verified
        results.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><Term id="dedup">Deduplication</Term>.</strong> Payload-hash uniqueness plus
        nonce/timestamp windows keep the same result from being submitted twice.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="ci-runs">CI runs</h2>
    <p>
      aiMark's own continuous-integration pipelines submit real runs to test the system end to end.
      These are labelled <Term id="ci-run">source=ci</Term> and
      <strong>excluded from default leaderboards</strong>: shared CI machines are slow, noisy, and
      say nothing about real hardware. They are not deleted — every board has a "show CI runs"
      toggle, and CI rows carry a visible badge wherever they appear. The one deliberate exception
      is the <a href="/cloud/">cloud monitor</a>, where CI-sourced probes are included by design —
      see <a href="#cloud-monitoring">cloud APIs are monitoring</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="protocol">Protocol details</h2>
    <p>
      Each suite fixes its prompts and decoding parameters (<Term id="temperature"
        >temperature</Term
      > 0 where determinism matters, fixed <Term id="max-tokens">max tokens</Term>), discards
      <Term id="warmup">warmup</Term> requests so <Term id="cold-start">cold-start</Term> costs
      don't pollute timing, runs at least 20 measured <Term id="repetition">repetitions</Term>, and
      enforces request timeouts. <Term id="ttft">TTFT</Term> is measured at the stream-event level —
      the timestamp of the first content token — with a separate network round-trip preflight so hosted
      numbers can be interpreted fairly.
    </p>

    <p class="muted">
      Want the exact numbers? Every suite manifest — tasks, weights, references, bounds — is public
      in the <a href="https://github.com/nsheaps/aiMark">aiMark repository</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
